Z||000001||Jefrysuko||05-24-2001||03:43 AM||||Those are the wheels that were on the Pace cars and were made by american racing.  I heard of someone calling and asking about them and said that they still had the ability to produce the rims but would charge an outrageous price.  I love the rims also but remember after hearing the price thinking it would have to wait until I won the lottery.<P>Another note:  They had a different bolt pattern than the Fiero.  If I remember right the pace cars used a modified bearing from a 2000 which changed the bolt pattern.   But, if you were getting them coustom made I don't see why you couldn't get the bolt pattern you want.<P>------------------<BR>Jeffrey D Suckow<BR>88 GT<BR>   <A HREF="mailto:Jefrysuko@aol.com">Jefrysuko@aol.com</A>  <p>[This message has been edited by Jefrysuko (edited 05-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Raydar||05-24-2001||07:36 AM||||The mid-80's Dodge Daytona had a wheel that  looks "close" to the pace car wheels.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 black Formula<BR>88 Duke coupe <BR>88 "wadded up" Formula (parts car)||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||05-24-2001||10:32 AM||||The wheels used on the IMSA and Real Pace cars was not from Americam Racing. They are from "Centerline" the style is called "Q". The AR's are just look alikes. The real ceneterlines were 15" wheels. The A/R only came in 14".<BR>The bolt pattern on the IMSA and Pace cars was the same as a Firebird. They used the F-body brakes and rotors.<P>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">     <BR><B><I>Got Emblems?</I></B><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 05-24-2001).]||||reg||1
